Imphal -\nMoirang-Khongjom-\nMoreh \n2015-16 89.66 \n5 North-\nEast \nIndia \nCircuit \nSikkim Development of \nTourist  Circuit linking \n- Rangpo     (entry) - \nRorathang -  Aritar -  \nPhadamchen - Nathang \n- Sherathang - \nTsongmo - Gangtok - \nPhodong - Mangan - \nLachung  - Yumthang - \nLachen - Thangu -   \nGurudongmer - Mangan \n- Gangtok - Tumin \nLingee - Singtam (exit) \nin Sikkim \n2015-16 98.05 \n\n6 Eco \nCircuit \nUttarakhand Integrated \nDevelopment of Eco -\nTourism, Adven ture \nSports, Associated \nTourism related \nInfrastructure for \nDevelopment of Tehri \nLake & Surroundings \nas New Destination -\nDistrict Tehri, \nUttarakhand \n2015-16 80.37 \n7 Desert \nCircuit \nRajasthan Development of \nSambhar Lake Town \nand Other Destinations \nunder Desert Circuit in \nSwadesh Darshan \nScheme \n2015-16 63.96 \n8 Tribal \nCircuit \nNagaland Development of Tribal \nCircuit Peren –Kohima-\nWokha, Nagaland \n2015-16 97.36 \n                 Grand   Total  582.40 \n \n \n                                                 *********** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_105": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.105 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2015 \n \nHigher Education and Research \n \n*105.  DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE: \n SHRI RAHUL SHEWALE:  \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether India and Australia propose to intensify existing partnership in    \nhigher education and research including technical and professional \neducation, schools, vocational education and training in the country; \n(b)  if so, whether India and Australia have signed a Memorandum of  \nUnderstanding (MoU) on cooperation in the fields of education, training \nand research in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the number of students likely to be benefited as a result thereof; and \n(d) whether India has signed similar MoUs with other countries and if so, \nthe details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the table of the House.  \n \n***** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 105 FOR 07.12.2015 ASKED BY HON’BLE MEMBERS OF \nPARLIAMENT DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE AND SHRI RAHUL SHEWALE \nREGARDING HIGHER EDUCATION AND RESEARCH \n \n(a)  India and Australia have had a very active bilateral cooperation in the field of \neducation. An Educational Exchange Programme(EEP) was signed between the two \ncountries way back in 2003. Under this, b oth countries have agreed on a detailed \nprogram of academic exchange under the EEP. Besides this, a Memorandum of \nCooperation (MoC) on Student Mobility and Welfare was signed between India and \nAustralia on 17\nth October, 2012. Further, with a view to give a strategic direction of \nthe education, training and research partnership between the two countries a bi-\nnational body Australia India Educational Council (AIEC) was established in 2011. \n \n(b)  A Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) on cooperation in the fields of \nEducation, Training and Research was signed on 24\nth August, 2015 at New Delhi \nduring the third Australia-India Education Council meeting. The objective of the MoU, \ninter alia, includes increase in cooperation across all education sectors – schools, \nvocational education and training (VET) and higher education including technical and \nprofessional education.  \n \n(c) Outcome of the MoU in terms of students benefitted is not quantifiable at this \njuncture.   \n \n(d)  Yes, Madam. The Government has signed Educational Exchange \nProgrammes (EEPs) / Memorandum of Unde rstandings (MoUs)/Joint Statements \nwith 53 countries/ multilateral organisations. The list of inter-governmental \ninstruments signed is annexed. \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 105 FOR 07.12.2015 \nASKED BY HON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT DR. SHRIKANT EKNATH SHINDE AND SHRI RAHUL \nSHEWALE REGARDING HIGHER EDUCATION AND RESEARCH \n \n1. MONGOLIA 09.07.2002 (EEP) \n2. ARMENIA  03.10.2002 (EEP) \n3. TANZANIA 27.04.2003 (EEP) \n4. GUYANA 25.08.2003 (EEP) \n5. ISRAEL 09.09.2003 (EEP) \n6. AUSTRALIA 23.10.2003 (EEP) \n24.08.2015 (MOU) \n7. MYANMAR 02.11.2003 (MOU) \n8. SYRIA 15.11.2003 (EEP) \n9. UZBEKISTAN 05.04.2005 (EEP) \n10. NEW ZEALAND 19.04.2005 (EEP) \n11. THAILAND 03.06.2005  (MOU) \n12. SRI LANKA 10.06.2005 (EEP) \n13. MEXICO 21.10.2005 (EEP) \n14. BRAZIL 01.02.2006 (EEP) \n15. AFGHANISTAN 10.04.2006 (MOU) \n16. CROATIA 19.04.2006 (EEP) \n17. ECUADOR 18.07.2006 (EEP) \n18. RWANDA 25.07.2006  (EEP) \n19. SOUTH AFRICA 02.10.2006 (MOU) \n20. SAUDI ARABIA 06.11.2006 (MOU) \n21. CHINA 15.05.2015 (EEP) \n22. PORTUGAL 11.01.2007 (EEP) \n23. FRANCE 15.02.2007 (EEP) \n24. ETHIOPIA 05.07.2007 (EEP) \n25. VIETNAM 06.07.2007 (EEP)  \n26. OMAN 14.12.2007 (MOU) \n27. NORWAY 18.06.2008(MOU) \n28. CHILE 17.03.2009 (EEP) \n29. BOTSWANA 09.01.2010 (EEP) \n30. MALAYSIA 20.1.2010 (MOU) \n31. TURKMENISTAN 25.05.2010 (MOU) \n32. CANADA 27.06.2010 (MOU) \n33. INDONESIA  25.01.2011 (MOU) \n34. MOZAMBIQUE 13.07.2011 (EEP) \n35. RUSSIA  16.12.2011 (EEP) \n36. TRINIDAD & TOBAGO 06.01.2012 (MOU) \n37. MAURITIUS  07.02.2012 (EEP) \n38. YEMEN 02.04.2012 (EEP) \n39. QATAR 09.04.2012 (EEP) \n40. TAJIKISTAN 03.09.2012 (EEP) \n41. BURUNDI 18.09.2012 (EEP) \n42. BELARUS 14.11.2012 (EEP) \n43. REPUBLIC OF KOREA 12.12.2012 (MOU) \n44. GERMANY 11.04.2013 (MOU) \n GERMANY 05.10.2015 (JDI)\n45. ESTONIA 15.10.2013 (EEP) \n46. PERU 28.10.2013 (EEP) \n47. KUWAIT 08.11.2013 (EEP) \n48. HUNGARY 19.11.2014 (EEP) \n49 U. K. Joint Statement (13.11.2014) \n50. U.S.A. Joint Statement (13.10.2011) \n23.01.2015 (JDI) \n51. CZECH REPUBLIC Joint Declaration (28.04.2009) \n52. U.A.E 03.09.2015 (MOU) \n53. BRICS Countries 18.11.2015 (MOU) \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_106": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 106 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2015 \n \nHOUSING FOR DAILY WAGE LABOURERS \n \n106. SHRI R.P. MARUTHARAJAA: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)whether the Government has formulated any housing scheme \nparticularly for the daily wage labourers in the country; \n(b)if so, the details thereof, State-wise; \n(c)the time by which the said proposal is likely to be implemented; and \n(d)if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a): No, Ministry of Labour & Employment has not formulated any \nhousing scheme particularly for daily wage labourers in the country. \nHowever, the Revised Integrated Housing Scheme (RIHS), 2007 for \nbeedi workers is being implemented in the country through the 17 \nWelfare Commissioners of the Labour Welfare Organisation of the \nMinistry. The housing subsidy of Rs. 40,000/ - is given in two equal \ninstallments to the workers for construction of houses.  \n(b) Not applicable. \n(c) Not applicable. \n(d) Not applicable. \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_107": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT OF INDUSTRIAL POLICY & PROMOTION \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 107. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, THE 30TH NOVEMBER, 2015. \n \n \nPROBLEMS FACED BY INDUSTRIES IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n107.    SHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                      वाणिज्य एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the industries in Maharashtra have been facing  immense problems \nand suffer due to high power tarrifs; and \n(b)  if so, the steps taken/being taken by the Government to ensure that industrial \nunits do not suffer because of this? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्‍ य‍एवं‍उद्योग‍ााज्‍ यमंत्री‍स्‍‍ वतंंत्र‍्रभााा(स्ीमतंी‍िरममलाा‍ीीतंाामि(‍ \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRIMATI NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a): The power tariff is decided by the Regulatory Authority i.e. Maharashtra \nElectricity Regulatory Commission (MERC).  However, as a stake \nholder Government of Maharashtra impresses up on MERC from time \nto time for fixing up reasonable power tariff. \n \n(b): The Government of Maharashtra has already taken steps against high \npower tariff by giving power tariff subsidy to eligible new Micro, Small \nand Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) and exemption from electricity duty \nto certain category of industrial units and units established in No -\nIndustry Districts and Naxalism affected areas.  \n \n*****   ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_108": "LOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 108 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2015 \n  \nDEMAND AND SUPPLY OF CNG  \n \n* 108.     SHRI DHARAM VIRA:   \n  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=h \n \n(a) the quantum of CNG produced in the country during the last three years, year -wise and \nState/UT-wise;  \n \n(b) whether the production of CNG is inadequate to meet the demand and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) the names of the cities where CNG is likely to be provided in the near future; and  \n \n(d) the measures taken by the Government to provide CNG in various cities and increase the \nproduction in a time-bound manner? \n \nANSWER \n \nपेट्रोलियम और  प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज् य मंत्ररी  स्‍  िंत्र प्राार  \nसश्ररी   धमेन्‍द र प्रधा      \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.108 ASKED BY SHRI DHARAM VIRA, MP TO BE \nANSWERED ON DECEMBER 7, 2015 \n \n(a):  Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) means natural gas used as fuel for vehicles typically \ncompressed to a pressure ranging from 200 -250 Bars in gaseous state. The quantum of CNG \nconsumed in the country during the last three Years, Years wise and State/UT -wise is as \nunder: \n(in MMSCMD) \nSl No. State/ UT 2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 0.054 0.051 0.059 \n2 Gujarat 1.635 1.696 1.804 \n3 Haryana 0.264 0.285 0.275 \n4 Madhya Pradesh 0.057 0.060 0.067 \n5 Maharashtra 1.707 1.939 2.088 \n6 Delhi 2.642 2.674 2.778 \n7 Rajasthan 0.004 0.006 0.010 \n8 Telangana 0.043 0.044 0.046 \n9 Tripura 0.018 0.025 0.035 \n10 Uttar Pradesh 0.540 0.650 0.739 \n  Total 6.964 7.432 7.900 \n \n(b):  In order to ensure adequate supply of domestic gas to CGD sector for CNG (transport) \nand PNG (domestic) segments MoP&NG  has notified guidelines on 20.8.2014 for allocation \nof domestic gas to CGD entities for CNG (transport) and PNG (domestic).  As per these \nguidelines, GAIL has been authorized to divert domestic gas from non -priority sector for \nsupply at uniform base price  to all authorized CGD entities for meeting their 100% \nrequirement in CNG (Transport) and PNG (Domestic) segments with a flexibility of \nadditional 10% of preceeding half year consumption.  For making above supply last half year \nconsumption in CNG (transpor t) and PNG (domestic) segments of individual CGD entity is \ntaken as base. The supply made to CGD sector has been kept in no cut category and the \nallocation of domestic gas for CNG (transport) and PNG (domestic) to CGD entity is being \nreviewed by GAIL in consultation with PPAC every six months. \n \n(c) & (d): In year 2007, Government has established Petroleum and Natural Gas \nRegulatory Board (PNGRB) under the PNGRB Act 2006.  Under the Act, PNGRB grants the \nauthorization to the entities for developing a City Ga s Distribution (CGD) network in a \n\nspecified Geographical Area (GA) of the country. CGD network supplies gas to four distinct \nsegments – Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) predominantly used as auto -fuel, and Piped \nNatural Gas (PNG) used in domestic, commercial a nd Industrial segments. At present, 67 \nCity Gas Distribution (CGD) networks are either in operation or under development by 25 \nCGD companies. More GAs are under bidding process to be authorized soon for spreading \nthe network in the length and breadth of co untry. Further, PNGRB has also issued 18 No \nObjection Certificates (NoCs) to 17 entities for setting up facilities for selling Compressed \nNatural Gas (CNG) for the areas where PNGRB has not yet authorized any entity for \ndeveloping or operating CGD network.  In order to further expand the coverage of CGD \nNetwork, PNGRB has initiated the 6 th round of CGD bidding for another 34 new \nGAs/Districts, which have nearby natural gas pipeline connectivity the List of the above \nmentioned 34 GAs is placed at Annexure-I. PNGRB has identified another 105 \nGAs/Districts that could have natural gas pipeline connectivity in future and could be \nconsidered for inclusion in future bidding rounds. The List of 105 Probable GAs has been \nplaced at Annexure-II. \n \n \n***** \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-I REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (c) & (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 108 TO BE ANSWERED ON 7.12.2015 REGARDING DEMAND AND \nSUPPLY OF CNG   \n \n \nList of GAs Covered under 6th CGD Bidding Round of PNGRB-  \n \n6th Round \n1. Dhar  (Madhya Pradesh) \n2. Shahdol (Madhya Pradesh) \n3. Jhabua (Madhya Pradesh) \n4. Datia (Madhya Pradesh) \n5. Rewa  (Madhya Pradesh) \n6. Dahod  (Gujarat) \n7. Banaskantha (Gujarat) \n8. Amreli (Gujarat) \n9. Patan (Gujarat) \n10. Dahej-Vagra Taluka (Gujarat) \n11. Ahmedabad (excluding the area \nalready authorized) (Gujarat) \n12. Anand (excluding the area already \nauthorized) (Gujarat) \n13. Panchmahal (Gujarat) \n14. Ahmadnagar (Maharashtra) \n15. Ratnagiri (Maharashtra) \n16. Saharanpur (Uttar Pradesh) \n17. Raebareli (Uttar Pradesh) \n18. Mainpuri  (Uttar Pradesh) \n19. Auraiya (Uttar Pradesh) \n20. Baghpat (Uttar Pradesh) \n21. Etawah  (Uttar Pradesh) \n22. Ramabai Nagar (Uttar Pradesh) \n23. Amethi  (Uttar Pradesh) \n24. Bhiwani  (Haryana) \n25. Yamunanagar (Haryana) \n26. Rewari  (Haryana) \n27. Rohtak  (Haryana) \n28. Bhatinda  (Punjab) \n29. Rupnagar (Punjab) \n30. Fatehgarh Sahib (Punjab) \n31. Gadag (Karnataka) \n32. Chitradurga (Karnataka) \n33. Nainital (Uttarakhand) \n34. North Goa (Goa) \n \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE-II REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART (c) & (d) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 108 TO BE ANSWERED ON 7.12.2015 REGARDING DEMAND AND \nSUPPLY OF CNG   \n \n \nThere are probable GAs (Districts) which could be connected  through Natural Gas \nPipelines (upcoming / under implementation) for future CGD bidding rounds \n \n \nS.No District State S.No District State \n1 Burdwan West Bengal 42 Cuttack Odisha \n2 East Singhbhum West Bengal 43 Dhenkanal Odisha \n3 Hoogly West Bengal 44 Jagatsinghpur Odisha \n4 Murshidabad West Bengal 45 Jajpur Odisha \n5 Purba Medinipur West Bengal 46 Kendrapara Odisha \n6 Purulia West Bengal 47 Khordha Odisha \n7 Gorakhpur Uttar Pradesh 48 Nauparha Odisha \n8 Varanasi Uttar Pradesh 49 Sambhalpur Odisha \n9 Adilabad Telangana 50 Sonapur Odisha \n10 Karimnagar Telangana 51 Amravati Maharashtra \n11 Chennai Tamil Nadu 52 Chandrapur Maharashtra \n12 Tiruvallur Tamil Nadu 53 Wardha Maharashtra \n13 Coimbatore Tamil Nadu 54 Akola Maharashtra \n14 Dharampur Tamil Nadu 55 Bhandra Maharashtra \n15 Erode Tamil Nadu 56 Buldhana Maharashtra \n16 Salem Tamil Nadu 57 Gondia Maharashtra \n17 Pratapgarh Rajasthan 58 Jalgaon Maharashtra \n18 Ajmer Rajasthan 59 Nashik Maharashtra \n19 Alwar Rajasthan 60 Betul Madhya Pradesh \n20 Bhilwara Rajasthan 61 Bhopal Madhya Pradesh \n21 Chhittorgarh Rajasthan 62 Hoshangabad Madhya Pradesh \n22 Churu Rajasthan 63 Mandsore Madhya Pradesh \n23 Hanumangarh Rajasthan 64 Neemuch Madhya Pradesh \n24 Jaipur Rajasthan 65 Rajgarh Madhya Pradesh \n25 Jhunjhunu Rajasthan 66 Ratlam Madhya Pradesh \n26 Jodhpur Rajasthan 67 Sagar Madhya Pradesh \n27 Pali Rajasthan 68 Sehore Madhya Pradesh \n28 Rajsamand Rajasthan 69 Vidisha Madhya Pradesh \n29 Sikar Rajasthan 70 Alappuzha Kerala \n30 Sirohi Rajasthan 71 Kannur Kerala \n31 Udaipur Rajasthan 72 Kasaragod Kerala \n32 Firozpur Punjab 73 Kozhikode Kerala \n33 Gurdaspur Punjab 74 Mallappuram Kerala \n34 Hoshiarpur Punjab 75 Palakkad Kerala \n35 Kapurthala Punjab 76 Thrissur Kerala \n36 Mansa Punjab 77 Chamrajnagar Karnataka \n37 Moga Punjab 78 Dakshini Kannad Karnataka \n38 Pathankot Punjab 79 Mandya Karnataka \n\n39 Angul Odisha 80 Bokaro Jharkhand \n40 Balangir Odisha 81 Dhanbad Jharkhand \n41 Bargarh Odisha 82 Ranchi Jharkhand \n83 Jammu Jammu & Kashmir 95 Gaya Bihar \n84 Kathua Jammu & Kashmir 96 Gopalganj Bihar \n85 Samba Jammu & Kashmir 97 Patna Bihar \n86 Srinagar Jammu & Kashmir 98 Saran Bihar \n87 Fatehabad Haryana 99 Siwan Bihar \n88 Sirsa Haryana 100 West Champaran Bihar \n89 Durg Chattisgarh 101 Nellore Andhra Pradesh \n90 Mahasumand Chattisgarh 102 Srikakulam Andhra Pradesh \n91 Raipur Chattisgarh 103 Vishakhapatnam Andhra Pradesh \n92 Rajnand Gaon Chattisgarh 104 Vizianagaram Andhra Pradesh \n93 Barh Bihar Bihar 105 Chittoor Andhra Pradesh \n94 Begusarai Bihar    \n \n \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n \n ",
